ve severe covet symptoms  so about 40 percent of patients in the  hospitals who have tested positive for  covet are not actually being treated for  covid they are what we call incidental  cases now they can add some additional  pressure to the system uh but to put  this in context with about 1500 overall  non-icu covet patients  actually only about 900 of them are  being treated for covet or covet as a  secondary condition  it has also and always been the  government's approach to keep public  health measures in place  only so long as they are absolutely  necessary to protect public health and  our health care system  throughout the pandemic alberta's policy  response is always sought to avoid  overwhelming our health system while  minimizing the damaging impact of  restrictions on the broader health of  our society  earlier in the pandemic when vaccine  uptake was much lower  other treatments weren't available and  testing was much harder to come by  so more drastic government interventions  were necessary to keep people safe  and let me just pause to address what  happened in alberta during the delta  wave in august and september the reality  is that we simply didn't have high  enough vaccine rates nor did our  neighbors in saskatchewan that's why we  were hit harder  but all of that has changed now 9 out of  10 albertans over the age of 12 have  some level of vaccine protection  antiviral drug treatments to prevent  severe disease and hospitalizations are  now available and more will come on  stream throughout the year ahead  in other words with what we know now  about the virus and the tools we have to  fight it the threat of covet 19  to public health is no no longer  outweighs  the hugely damaging impact of health  restrictions on our society on people's  mental health on their emotional  well-being on uh  our broader social health  so now is the time to begin learning to  live with kovid  these restrictions have led to terrible  division even amongst families